{
  "dom_id": "step-q125252",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Find the value of $c$ that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x)={log}_{3}\u2061x$ on the interval $[9,81].$",
  "worked_solution": "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[9,81]$ and differentiable on $(9,81),$ hence, we can apply the Mean Value Theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (9,81)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=9$ and $b=81$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(c) & =\\frac{f(81)-f(9)}{81-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{3}\u2061(81)-{log}_{3}\u2061(9)}{72} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-2}{72} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{3}\u2061x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}\u20613}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(c)=\\frac{1}{c\\operatorname{ln}\u20613}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{c\\operatorname{ln}\u20613} & =\\frac{1}{36} \\\\ c & =\\frac{36}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20613}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
